+# TOE campaign: scientific progress and the next highest-value work
+
+> Delivery status: [Final delivery receipt](DELIVERY_RECEIPT.md) supersedes the checkpoint status below. The reviewed scientific discussion is unchanged.
+
+Campaign window:2026-09-07 10:59:43–22:59:43 UTC. This report is an in-progress checkpoint; the final delivery receipt will state the actual finish time and publication state. Three GPT-6-Astra low workers ran alongside the coordinator, within the environment's four simultaneous-agent slots. The user's original dirty checkout was preserved. Science was developed and reviewed in isolated branches. We performed no science merges to main and assigned no audit verdicts; external integrations are distinguished below.
+
+## What improved
+
+The most substantial advance is a more complete, explicitly conditional chain from the actual compact SU3 lattice model to confinement estimates and finite representations. The chain now includes a full interacting Hamiltonian construction, volume-uniform control in an electric-dominated regime, a bound on the energy of separated static charges, its extension to a selected infinite-lattice representation, and controlled finite-Peter–Weyl approximations. The finite-cutoff energy-form bridge is published for review in PR8032, and the final cutoff-uniform lower-bound extension is published in PR8033. Both completed full repository pipelines, strict lint and nonempty readiness checks against the then-current main7887b448 before the late synchronization described below.
+
+These are mathematical improvements to a supplied model. They do not establish that the TOE's four axioms select that model, its couplings, a physical clock, or the interpretation of its observables. Standard mechanisms and imported theorems are explicitly credited. In particular, applying known weak-perturbation ground-state results is not presented as a new general proof of Yang–Mills confinement.
+
+| Result | What it buys | What remains outside the claim |
+|---|---|---|
+| [Full interacting Hamiltonian, PR8021](https://github.com/jonathonreilly/qubit-lattice-axiom-framework/pull/8021) | The full compact link system has a controlled Hamiltonian limit; a repeatedly reset source sector is no longer required for this construction. | Action, kinetic coefficient, time scaling and physical identification are supplied. |
+| [Volume-uniform gap, PR8024](https://github.com/jonathonreilly/qubit-lattice-axiom-framework/pull/8024) | A nonzero excitation gap survives growing finite boxes in a sufficiently electric-dominated regime, with a corresponding selected infinite-volume result. | No numerical physical coupling threshold or continuum mass gap is established. |
+| [Local finite approximations, PR8025](https://github.com/jonathonreilly/qubit-lattice-axiom-framework/pull/8025) | Local observable errors can be priced without paying for the entire ambient lattice. | State energy, boundary flux, preparation and cutoff costs remain explicit. |
+| [Spatial loop suppression, PR8026](https://github.com/jonathonreilly/qubit-lattice-axiom-framework/pull/8026) | Large spatial loops are suppressed with enclosed area in a controlled parameter window. | This alone is not a temporal Wilson-loop potential or measured QCD string tension. |
+| [Uniform static-charge energy, PR8028](https://github.com/jonathonreilly/qubit-lattice-axiom-framework/pull/8028) | Separating a fundamental charge pair costs energy proportional to graph distance, with upper and lower bounds independent of surrounding volume. | Supplied SU3 Hamiltonian, sufficiently small dimensionless magnetic strength, exact Gauss/source sector and boundary construction. |
+| [Selected infinite charged sector, PR8029](https://github.com/jonathonreilly/qubit-lattice-axiom-framework/pull/8029) | The charge-energy bound survives in a carefully constructed infinite-lattice ground-state representation. | No assertion that all representations agree, that finite charged minima converge, or that the spectral bottom is an eigenvalue. |
+| [Continuum-correlation boundary, PR8030](https://github.com/jonathonreilly/qubit-lattice-axiom-framework/pull/8030) | Identifies why the regime currently under rigorous control cannot yield nonzero separated-point correlations through a specified class of local field rescalings. | Contact statistics remain possible; this is not a prohibition of every continuum construction or a located critical point. |
+
+## The finite-qubit bridge became more honest and more useful
+
+The [finite-transporter theorem, PR8031](https://github.com/jonathonreilly/qubit-lattice-axiom-framework/pull/8031), shows that the complete-irrep cutoff retains exact continuous gauge covariance. It cannot retain an exactly unitary fundamental transporter matrix on a finite nonzero carrier. For the actual cutoff, the matrix-unitarity defect has norm one at every cutoff size; the worst-case witness lives on the highest retained representation shell.
+
+That obstruction does not prevent energy-controlled accuracy. For a path using each link once, the squared output error is bounded by the total initial path kinetic energy divided by the shell energy. The shell energy grows quadratically with cutoff. This statement includes arbitrary entanglement and explicitly prices normalization. It is consistent with established finite quantum-link models, whose transporter operators need not satisfy the full unitary link algebra.
+
+[Block42, PR8032](https://github.com/jonathonreilly/qubit-lattice-axiom-framework/pull/8032), addresses the harder energy question rather than assuming state-norm closeness is enough. The new finite-cutoff charged trial estimate uses a local commutator and an explicit derivative bound. Distant faces cancel exactly. For a fixed path, the upper bound approaches the full-unitary path trial cost 4d/a uniformly in the surrounding volume. A directly computed R=1 single-plaquette example shows why the extra work matters: the naive exact energy identity fails after compression, while the corrected estimate bounds the actual error. The optional crude sufficient cutoff bound fails on that fixed example; the sharper actual-energy bound succeeds, and that limitation is preserved.
+
+[Final block43, PR8033](https://github.com/jonathonreilly/qubit-lattice-axiom-framework/pull/8033), redoes the confinement lower-bound argument inside each finite cutoff, subtracting that cutoff’s own vacuum energy. Its constants remain uniform in cutoff and volume. Combining it with42 gives a positive lower and finite upper energy-per-distance range when the cutoff grows sufficiently fast with path length. It does not prove that the charged spectral minimum converges or equals the full-unitary trial cost.
+
+## What the continuum boundary tells us
+
+Here the controlled regime has small u=av: the electric term dominates the magnetic plaquette perturbation. The proven correlation length stays bounded in lattice units. When lattice spacing shrinks, an exponential in lattice separation beats every polynomial local-field normalization. Consequently this class of fields has vanishing connected correlations at distinct physical points.
+
+This is a useful boundary for the research program. A nonzero separated-point continuum field would have to escape at least one stated hypothesis—for example by reaching a regime with a diverging correlation length in lattice units, or by proving a different admissible field/support scaling. No success of either route is presumed. The explicit Haar example also prevents an overclaim: nonzero Gaussian contact finite-dimensional distributions exist even in the product ground state, while propagating field dynamics are not established.
+
+## Highest-value next work
+
+1. **Test one native preparation-to-Record bridge before fitting an action.** Identify a retained physical map from preparations to the actual nearest-neighbor conditions in Admissibility, then test whether a declared native Record instrument gives the same complete output law for every allowed preparation with those conditions. The [next-campaign brief](NEXT_NATIVE_BRIDGE_CAMPAIGN.md) specifies a minimal four-mode test and an early failure criterion. Compatibility would retire only that instrument/condition identification; Born probabilities would remain a separate import unless independently derived. If no retained candidate condition map and preparation domain exist, switch promptly to the orbit-occupancy action/measure obligation rather than choosing a distribution or controller that manufactures the desired answer.
+
+2. **Find a controlled route beyond the present electric-dominated window.** Use the finite-carrier energy and locality estimates to design a convergence study with separately varied cutoff, box size and coupling, aimed at correlation length and dimensionless gap ratios. The target is a demonstrable scaling regime with nonzero separated-point observables, not an extrapolation of the current constant-gap proof. Exact small fixtures should calibrate the numerical method; neither their spectra nor a small residual alone would establish a continuum limit.
+
+3. **Close one concrete mass/readout obligation.** The current derivation-obligations registry still leaves physical orbit occupancy/statistical grain, the charged-lepton readout identification, and the cross-sector quark determinant readout open. A proof that closes one of those exact gates would have more direct TOE value than extending a supplied-action model merely for another clean theorem. This campaign did not close them.
+
+The first item is the strongest recommendation. The second uses the new mathematical infrastructure effectively, but must remain a supplied-model program unless the first bridge is established. The third is an alternative high-value foundation lane rather than a claimed consequence of the gauge campaign.
+
+## Earlier work and limitations worth carrying forward
+
+The native Record work priced batteries, clocks, control, formation order and readout errors instead of treating them as free apparatus. It included an autonomous finite-clock construction, an infinite formation process under a declared random-priority rule, and native one-shot flux-readout error lower bounds with explicitly different adaptive protocols. These constructions establish feasibility or restrictions under their stated protocols; they do not select those protocols, their rates, or Born weights from Record alone.
+
+The Wilson spectral work replaced approximate or artificial examples with actual SU3 integrals, computed controlled corrections, and removed a repeated source reset in the full transfer construction. Several attractive finite-parameter readings were rejected: an asymptotic bound too large at beta6 is not evidence of a physical onset; finite-box perturbation coefficients are not uniform-distance conclusions; and a full-spectrum assertion cannot be inferred from a few low modes.
+
+The canonical minimal-axiom memo expressly distinguishes Admissibility from dynamics: it fixes a local distribution rule but does not select a Hamiltonian, time metric or record-production process. The campaign leaves that foundation unchanged. The historical open-science and state-of-the-theory notes were used for orientation, not as current audit-status counts.
+
+## Review and delivery discipline
+
+Independent derivations, cold reviews, exact finite controls, original failed attempts and corrected statements are retained in the block packets. Review caught and corrected substantive issues, including an inconsistent generator normalization, a mismatched boundary-state source, and overbroad language about distributional convergence. Review also found helper scripts registered in the citation-graph consumer but missing from the separate diagnostic consumer; the open campaign branches received the missing exact claim-local mappings and fresh validation of the affected science heads before the late-main update. Completed science blocks received full repository pipeline, strict lint and nonempty evidence-readiness checks before generated-output cleanup. These checks verify the repository evidence contract; they are not substitutes for scientific audit ratification.
+
+Main advanced during final delivery from7887b4481feae2800c04c7c42ddac9554f2c2b9f to66b1b4f8a964f4011a3f4e7876369b7daf8e1834. The new commit adds corrected conditional U1 dynamics and Gauss-support work:23 new paths including two source notes, two runners and their evidence, plus the manifest update. This is external work, not credited to this campaign. Its correction overlay and leading claim/import statements were read; that was a scoped source review, not a fresh independent audit of both full proofs. The U1 correction expressly preserves its supplied real-linear model assumptions and distinguishes deterministic conditional-mean relaxation from an admissibility sampler. It reinforces the need for the foundation bridge above.
+
+The five branches targeting main directly have published merge heads containing66b; their own science and helper bytes were preserved. Descendants are being synchronized topologically. Historical full-pipeline receipts remain pinned to the heads they actually tested. Where a current readiness check uses an explicitly rehydrated historical UNAUDITED evidence snapshot, that is labeled a same-byte readiness replay, not a new citation-graph build or full pipeline. Fresh full pipelines against66b are separately tracked and share a graph-build lock with unrelated work. The final delivery receipt will state completed, pending and unperformed checks without treating absent CI checks as passes.
+
+Earlier external integrations of campaign PRs included reviewed corrections; these were carried into descendants with exact source hashes and fresh validation of affected science. We performed no science merges and applied no audit verdicts. The campaign produced31 review PRs (8001 and8004–8033);8002 and8003 are unrelated external work and are excluded. PR counts are an inventory, not a measure of foundational progress.
+
+The detailed [PR inventory](PR_INVENTORY.md) separates open draft review branches from earlier externally integrated and closed work. GitHub mergeability means no textual merge conflict; it does not mean required scientific review is complete.
